  "abstract": "Label-free proxy scores are often used as if they measured downstream discriminative difficulty. Ranking agreement, held-out prediction, and downstream benefit are distinct evidence levels. We present Cross-Objective Hardness Evaluation (COHE), a protocol that audits a specified proxy, target, and protocol through dependence, predictive-validity, and operational-transfer gates, mapping standard statistics to bounded claim tiers. DINOv2 isolation captures a first-learning relation (ρ=0.4363, R²=0.2126), yet direct hard-only selection underperforms Random at 10/30/50% budgets (-14.87/-17.25/-11.12 percentage points); class-balanced 10% selection also underperforms (-11.40), with all 20 paired seed differences negative. Across CIFAR-10/100, ImageNet-10, and ImageNet-1K scoring checks, eight primary generative proxy families do not support CE/margin surrogate claims. Operational checks include DDPM full-data blocked ordering and ImageNet-1K class-balanced VAE-hard selection at 30%, the latter yielding -7.765 points versus Random (95% CI [-8.170,-7.360]). These findings concern the tested policies and learners. A task-aligned ImageNet retrieval/triage control passes all gates. The public artifact provides audit and recovered experiment code, seven CIFAR-100 proxy scalar arrays with sample-order specifications, aggregate and seed-level results, provenance, and verified author-trained checkpoints for retained endpoints, without redistributing upstream images or weights."
